  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1125 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThursday night through Monday:\n\nSynopsis: Upper level ridging will persist over the western CONUS \nthrough the period, with west-northwest upper level winds expected \nacross the western Great Lakes. High pressure will shift east Friday \ninto Saturday, allowing for southerly winds and steadily climbing \nsurface temperatures through the weekend. Increasing humidity will \neventually accompany the rising temperatures, though its arrival \nwill lag behind that of the warmer air temperatures. Expect that \ndeeper humidity will make it back into the area later Saturday. Low \npressure is progged to move quickly from the Canadian Prairies \nFriday into Saturday, pulling a cold front into the western Great \nLakes. The boundary is forecast to slowly advance southward through \nthe area Saturday night through Monday, with medium range guidance \nsplit over the speed of its progression. With the slow-moving front \nimpinging on the newly-arrived more humid air, periods of showers \nand thunderstorms return to the forecast from Saturday night into \nMonday. The aforementioned uncertainties regarding frontal \nprogression lead to low forecast confidence in preferred storm \ntiming & corridors at this time, with details coming into view as \ntrends clarify through the remainder of this week.  \n\nSaturday through Monday: Continues to appear that the most \nnoteworthy weather of the entire forecast period will occur during \nthis time frame, with returning heat/humidity & a slow-moving \nsurface front leading to elevated (90+ degF) heat indices and storm \nchances. Trends continue to point toward heat indices staying just \nbelow advisory thresholds, with the latest statistical guidance \nholding most > 50% probabilities of 100 degF heat indices to the \nwest-southwest of the area. Will continue to be watching for any \nupward trends. Blended solution of the NBM currently favors a Sunday \nafternoon & night timing for storms, implying that southern \nWisconsin will remain too capped for convection Saturday/Saturday \nnight & that the responsible frontal boundary will be too far south \nof the area to support additional development on Monday. Quick \ncomparison of individual ensemble & deterministic solutions, \nhowever, makes it clear that this timeline could shift with changes \nin frontal speed, mesoscale influences, or both. Thus anticipate \nsome additional shifts in the timing forecasts for the peak precip \nwindow(s) through the second half of this week. Raw overlap of \nmodeled instability, shear & precipitable water implies that there \ncould be some potential for strong/severe storms with heavy \ndownpours, though the aforementioned uncertainties make it difficult \nto offer additional details at this time. Will continue to provide \nupdates as trends clarify.\n\nQuigley",
